<TopicPage slug="subjects/real-estate/foundational/property-and-ownership">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Real estate's foundation is not dirt but a legal idea: ownership of land is not one right
    but a <strong>bundle of rights</strong> — to possess, to use, to exclude others, to
    transfer, and to encumber (borrow against) — and each right in the bundle can be held,
    separated, sold or limited independently. What a homeowner purchases, what a tenant rents,
    what a utility company crosses the garden with and what a bank holds as security are all
    different bundles tied to the same physical land. This abstraction is not academic: nearly
    every question in the field — what is being bought, what a lease conveys, what a mortgage
    actually is, why a neighbour can walk across your lot — is answered by asking which sticks
    of which bundle belong to whom.
  </p>
  <p>
    An example shows the concept doing work. A house sits on a lot with: a fee simple title in
    the owner's name; a 2-metre utility easement along one side (the power company's right of
    passage); a recorded deed restriction forbidding outbuildings; a mortgage in favour of a
    lender (their encumbrance, your debt); and a tenant in the basement flat on a one-year
    lease (their exclusive possession for the term). Five parties hold five different bundles
    in one property, each legally real and each priced differently. This page builds the
    vocabulary piece by piece: real versus personal property, the forms and estates of
    ownership, and the interests and encumbrances that shrink or shape the bundle.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bundle, the estates, and what title proves" widget="equationStep" config={bundleCfg} />

  <h2>Real property versus personal property — and the fixture problem</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Real property</strong> is land and everything permanently attached to it: the
    surface, the soil and minerals beneath (subject to severance), the airspace above, the
    buildings, and the <em>appurtenances</em> — rights that run with the land, such as water
    rights or an easement enjoyed over neighbouring land. <strong>Personal property</strong>
    (chattels) is everything else: furniture, vehicles, shares, tools. The boundary sounds
    clean until objects move across it, and the moving objects are called <strong>fixtures</strong>
    — items that began as personal property and became real by attachment. Is the chandelier
    part of the house (it conveys with the sale) or the seller's treasured chattel (she takes
    it down)? Is the fitted kitchen real property while the freestanding fridge is not? Law
    answers with tests, memorably bundled as <strong>MARIA</strong>: <em>Method</em> of
    attachment (bolted in or merely plugged in?), <em>Adaptability</em> to the property's use
    (is it custom-fitted to this building?), <em>Relationship</em> of the parties (a tenant's
    installation is treated differently from an owner's), <em>Intention</em> of the person who
    installed it, and any express <em>Agreement</em> — which is why contracts list exclusions.
    Two specialist carve-outs matter commercially: <strong>trade fixtures</strong> — a dentist's
    chair, a shop's counters — remain the business tenant's personal property even though
    bolted down, provided they are removed before the lease ends and damage is repaired; and
    crops (emblements) grown by a tenant remain personal property with a right to harvest.
    The fixture doctrine explains a loud truth of practice: real estate's disputes are rarely
    about the land itself — they are about <em>which bundle was promised</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Forms of ownership: estates, co-ownership and entities</h2>
  <p>
    Ownership is legally described by its <strong>estate</strong> — the duration and quality of
    the rights held. The freehold family: <strong>fee simple absolute</strong> is the full
    bundle, of indefinite duration, freely inheritable — what "owning a home" means to most
    people; <strong>fee simple defeasible</strong> (or conditional) is fee simple that ends or
    reverts if a stated condition is broken ("so long as the land is used for a school") — the
    grantor keeps a reversionary interest and conditions on titles still occasionally bite;
    the <strong>life estate</strong> lasts for a named person's lifetime, after which the
    property passes to a <em>remainderman</em> (the classic estate-planning tool: "house to my
    wife for life, then to my children" creates exactly this). The leasehold family is
    carved out of the fee: <strong>estates for years</strong> (a fixed term), <strong>periodic
    tenancies</strong> (month to month, renewing), <strong>tenancies at will</strong> and
    <strong>at sufferance</strong> (the holdover tenant) — all of which convey possession for
    time while the fee, and ultimately the reversion, stay with the landlord. Valuation
    follows the estate, not the dirt: a 99-year leasehold flat and a freehold flat on
    identical land are different assets with different prices — the leasehold's value
    decays as the term shortens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several people can hold the same estate simultaneously, and the legal form decides what
    happens when one of them dies. <strong>Tenancy in common</strong>: co-owners hold
    (possibly unequal) shares; a deceased co-owner's share passes to <em>her heirs</em>.
    <strong>Joint tenancy</strong>: created with the four unities (time, title, interest,
    possession) and the distinctive <strong>right of survivorship</strong> — a deceased joint
    tenant's share vanishes into the survivors automatically, outside the will and probate;
    the married-couple variant is <strong>tenancy by the entirety</strong> (some states) and
    community-property states have their own spousal regime. The difference is a worked
    example in itself: two siblings buy a cottage as joint tenants; one dies — the survivor
    owns the whole cottage, full stop. As tenants in common, the deceased sibling's half
    instead goes to her heirs, who may be strangers to the survivor and co-owners of a lawn
    mower they cannot divide. Beyond co-ownership stand the ownership <strong>entities</strong> —
    individual, partnership, LLC and corporation (which shield personal assets and
    fractionalise ownership), trusts (which separate control from benefit), and the
    <strong>condominium</strong> (fee simple ownership of a unit plus shared ownership of
    common elements) versus the <strong>co-operative</strong> (a corporation owning the
    building; residents own shares and a proprietary lease) — forms that decide who you sue,
    how you finance, and what happens on default.
  </p>

  <h2>Interests, easements and encumbrances: the sticks others hold</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>easement</strong> is the right to use another's land for a purpose — the least
    disruptive encumbrance and the most common. <strong>Appurtenant</strong> easements benefit
    a neighbouring parcel (a right of way to a landlocked lot — there is a dominant estate
    that enjoys the benefit and a servient estate that bears it) and run with the land;
    <strong>easements in gross</strong> benefit a person or company instead (the utility
    company's power-line corridor). Easements are created expressly in writing, or by
    implication (an obvious pre-existing use when land is divided), by <em>necessity</em>
    (the landlocked parcel's access), or by <strong>prescription</strong> — open, notorious,
    hostile and continuous use for the statutory period (often 10–20 years) ripens into a
    right, the same elements that make <strong>adverse possession</strong>, the doctrine by
    which a long, open, unchallenged occupation can ripen into ownership itself. A
    <strong>licence</strong> (permission to use, revocable — your cinema ticket) is the
    easement's weaker cousin and must not be confused with it. Beyond easements, the
    encumbrance family: <strong>liens</strong> — creditors' claims against the property
    (mortgages, unpaid taxes, mechanic's liens), ranked by priority, which is why the first
    mortgagee is repaid first in foreclosure and why tax liens leapfrog; <strong>private
    restrictions</strong> (covenants and HOA rules that bind successive owners); and
    <strong>public limits</strong> — zoning (the police power regulating use), building codes,
    and <strong>eminent domain</strong>, the state's power to take property for public use
    with just compensation. Every one of these removes a stick from the owner's bundle, and
    the market reprices each: an encumbered title is a different asset, which is why
    <strong>title examination</strong> exists — tracing the chain of recorded deeds, easements
    and liens so the buyer knows exactly which bundle is changing hands — and why
    <strong>title insurance</strong> underwrites the residue of doubt. Deeds come in grades
    matching the promise: a <strong>general warranty deed</strong> warrants the whole chain of
    title; a <strong>special warranty deed</strong> warrants only the grantor's own period;
    a <strong>quitclaim deed</strong> warrants nothing at all — it transfers whatever the
    grantor happens to have, which might be nothing, and is the instrument of choice for
    clearing clouds and settling disputes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "owning" land means owning everything above, below and around it absolutely. Every ownership is legally <em>bounded</em> — by zoning, easements, the rights of neighbours and the state's taxing and taking powers — and the professional skill (in appraisal, in brokerage, in law) is knowing precisely which rights are in the bundle being valued or conveyed. "The house" is never the whole story; the title is.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two worked miniatures show the doctrine priced in practice. The first is
    <strong>lien priority in foreclosure</strong>: a house worth $400,000 carries a $300,000
    first mortgage, a $50,000 second mortgage, and a $10,000 mechanics' lien recorded last
    but for work done first. Foreclosure distributes by priority, not by size: the first
    mortgagee is paid in full, the second takes what remains ($90,000 of its $50,000 claim —
    covered), and the mechanics' lien holder — depending on the jurisdiction's special
    priority rules — may find itself paid from dregs or nothing, which is why lenders
    require title searches and why subcontractors fight for statutory lien priority. The
    second is <strong>easement creation by prescription</strong>: a neighbour has driven
    across the corner of a lot to reach a garage for nineteen years, openly, without
    permission, while the owners said nothing; in a jurisdiction with a twenty-year period,
    one more year of the same use ripens a revocable permission into a permanent property
    right — and the owner who finally objects discovers that two decades of silence was the
    gift. Both miniatures teach the same lesson: interests are created by records, by
    statutes and by <em>conduct</em>, and the professional's job is knowing which clock is
    running.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper layers show where the foundation leads. <strong>Severed rights</strong>: the
    bundle's sticks can be split vertically as well as temporally — mineral estates sold
    beneath a retained surface (and the law of capture that governs migrating oil and gas),
    transferable development rights, billboard and solar air-rights leases — creating
    "split estates" whose valuation and disputes (whose is the fracking under the subdivision?)
    are a specialty of their own. <strong>Recording systems</strong>: the Anglo-American
    deed-recording regime makes title a matter of traced documents and priority rules —
    first to record wins, subject to exceptions — while the Torrens registration system
    (Australia and scattered jurisdictions) makes title conclusive by state guarantee; the
    choice of system shapes everything from conveyancing cost to fraud risk. <strong>Regulatory
    takings</strong>: the live constitutional question of how far public regulation (zoning,
    environmental limits, rent control) may shrink the bundle before it becomes a
    compensable "taking" — the line courts still draw case by case, and the reason the
    bundle-of-rights metaphor remains a live legal instrument rather than a classroom
    antique. The through-line for the practitioner is constant: real estate is the trade in
    <em>legal rights</em>, and every later topic this programme covers — valuation, finance,
    brokerage, land use — begins by asking the question this unit equips you to answer:
    which bundle, held by whom, with which sticks missing?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> national licensing exam content outline (ARELLO-aligned); Reilly, <em>The Language of Real Estate</em>; property-law treatises on estates and fixtures.</p>
